{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791031825,"nonce":"+7v/l+J2hR74WnkIO60BdRC3Rqw=","title":"\u003cfor\u003e lays its items out in a box of its own, a row by default","message":"hxx lowers \u003cfor\u003e to ashui.ui.For, which extends Div: its items are children of an extra flex node with default style (flex-direction row, no gap), not of the element the \u003cfor\u003e is written in. Inside a flex-col list the rows come out side by side, wrapping their text; the parent's gap, direction and alignment do not reach them.\n\nReproducer: \u003cdiv class=\"flex flex-col gap-1\" width={280}\u003e\u003cfor {i in [1, 2, 3]}\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"px-3\"\u003e\u003ctext\u003eRow ${i}\u003c/text\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/for\u003e\u003c/div\u003e lays the three rows in a row.\n\nTaffy has no display: contents, not in 0.6.3 (ours) nor in 0.14.0 (latest), so upgrading does not help. Fix in ashui: make For a fragment, not a node, and splice its items into the parent's child list at its place, updating that list when the items change. TextArea's \u003cfor\u003e loops only hold absolute children, which is why it went unnoticed.","files":null}]}